messages:
Maria H: Hey team — ACME Prod reported a sudden spike to 429s this morning during their nightly processing window. They say the errors start ~10s after the job kicks off. Request ID in logs: req_0a9b2f. Can someone triage? :eyes:

Ajay R: Do we have their telemetry? Q: concurrent requests and tokens per request? 429 body says 'quota_exhausted' or 'rate_limited'?

Maria H: 429 body shows quota_exhausted. From the sample logs they posted: burst_credit_balance: 8 -> 0 in ~30s. Job fires ~20 concurrent requests, each ~800-1k tokens. So peak token/sec ~15-20k.

Oliver L: That makes sense. Short bursts like that will drain burst credits quickly unless smoothing is on or they've got Dedicated capacity. Did they enable burst smoothing in Console?

Sara T: They didn't enable smoothing. Quick repro/check steps I suggested to them: 1) call our quota status endpoint to see 'burst_credit_balance' and 'smoothing_window', 2) add Request-Id header when hitting API so we can map logs, 3) capture a small sample of the job payloads. Curl to check quota:```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $REDWOOD_KEY" \\  \\
  https://api.redwood.ai/v1/account/usage/quota | jq
``` Response will include fields like "burst_credit_balance", "burst_refill_rate", "smoothing_enabled": true/false.

Maria H: They ran the curl and pasted the JSON — smoothing_enabled:false, burst_credit_balance:3, burst_refill_rate:600 tokens/sec, smoothing_window:0.

Ajay R: With 20 concurrent * ~900 tokens ≈ 18k tokens/sec, refill 600/s won't keep up. They hit zero in seconds. Two immediate options: short-term manual credit top-up (we can add temporary burst credits) or instruct them to reduce concurrency / add client-side smoothing/backoff. Longer-term: enable smoothing or increase baseline quota.

support-bot: I added a manual credit top-up: +2000 burst credits for org org-acme (expires in 12h) — ticket #SUP-8742. :white_check_mark:

Maria H: Thanks — I told the customer we added 2k credits as a temporary mitigation and to throttle to 5 concurrent while they implement smoothing. They asked for recommended smoothing settings.

Oliver L: Recommended smoothing config for this pattern: smoothing_window: 60s, burst_refill_rate ~2000 tokens/sec (aligns to their sustained throughput), max_burst_multiplier: 3-5 depending on allowed volatility. That turns instantaneous extremes into a token-bucket-smoothed flow. Example policy snippet for Console/Config:```json
{
  "smoothing_enabled": true,
  "smoothing_window_s": 60,
  "burst_refill_rate_tps": 2000,
  "max_burst_multiplier": 4
}
```

Sara T: For client-side quick fixes, recommend exponential backoff with jitter and local queueing. Example (JS SDK):```js
async function sendWithBackoff(req) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < 6) {
    try {
      return await redwood.generate(req);
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.code === 'quota_exhausted' || err.status === 429) {
        const base = Math.min(1000 * 2 ** attempt, 10000);
        const jitter = Math.random() * 300;
        await sleep(base + jitter);
        attempt++;
      } else throw err;
    }
  }
  throw new Error('exhausted retries');
}
``` Also reduce concurrency: they should batch smaller or pipeline streaming if possible to avoid huge per-request token spikes.

Maria H: Customer also asked about cost/billing implications of enabling smoothing. Oliver, can you confirm whether smoothing changes billing metering or just redistributes tokens over time?

Oliver L: Smoothing is a control layer only — it does not change how tokens are metered for billing. It just smooths admission into the runtime so you avoid transient quota exhaustion. Billing is still on consumed tokens.

Ajay R: One more troubleshooting note: ask the customer to include the request_id header for failed calls and attach a short log snippet. When they provided req_0a9b2f we saw machine logs showing:```
WARN auth: token OK
INFO requests: recv 20 concurrent
DEBUG quota: burst_credit_balance=8
DEBUG quota: serve_tokens=18000
ERROR quota: burst exhausted -> 429 req_0a9b2f
```
This maps to our earlier estimate.

Maria H: They're going to enable smoothing in Console (I walked them through Console → Settings → Quotas → Burst smoothing) and throttle to 5 concurrent while deploying client changes. They asked if we can monitor and revert the manual top-up early if not needed.

support-bot: Noted — I'll watch org-acme burst_credit_balance and alert if it stays high/unused. I'll also add a 24h follow-up reminder to SUP-8742.

Oliver L: I'll open an engineering follow-up to look at default burst allocations and whether we should auto-suggest smoothing for orgs with sustained short spikes. Tagging @eng-oncall to triage patterns and potential UX prompts.

eng-oncall: Ack — will take SUP-8742-ENG for analysis. Quick checks I'll run: 1) aggregate org usage histograms (peak per-second distribution), 2) frequency of short bursts vs sustained load, 3) whether burst refill_rate defaults are too conservative.

Maria H: Customer acceptance — they enabled smoothing and throttled client, errors dropped in their next run. They appreciated the temporary credits. I'll leave this thread open until ENG posts findings and we confirm whether to suggest auto-smoothing changes. :thumbsup:

Oliver L: Great. Action items: Maria — collect their post-change logs and close SUP-8742 when clean. Oliver — create ENG ticket and attach aggregated histograms. Ajay — draft a short KB article: "When to enable burst smoothing vs Dedicated capacity". Sara — add the example backoff snippet to the support KB.

Maria H: On it. Closing interim steps and will update once ENG posts. Thanks everyone! :raised_hands:
